5432:./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5459:orchestration/tickets/T15-day-review/impl-review-raw.txt:1545:    44	- Fields: `title`, `state` (Section 4.1), `priority` ∈ {P0..P3} (default P3), `deadline` (nullable date), `project` (same enum, nullable when parented — derived from parent's item), `sprint_item_id` (nullable), `sprint_id` (nullable; writable only when `sprint_item_id` is NULL — writes rejected otherwise; when parented it is derived from the parent item), `recap` (markdown, writable only when state is past `needs_success`; writes before that are rejected with a structured error), `ceiling` (a ticket state; Section 4.3), `at_cap` ∈ {`stop`, `propose`}, `auto_blocked` (bool, Section 7.5), `chat_session_key` (nullable string), `fields` (Section 4.2).
5453:./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5482:orchestration/tickets/T15-day-review/impl-review-raw.txt:1856:    91	**Why this is not a client-side state store:** the no-store rule (SPEC §9, T14 doctrine) forbids caching/reconciling **canonical server data**. Chat messages are never stored server-side — `POST /api/chat/{id}/send` returns `{reply_text, session_key}` and persists only the session key (chat/service.py:64-104). The transcript is pure conversation-render state that exists nowhere else; losing it on page reload is correct (refresh restores server state, and the server has no transcript). Module scope survives WS-flush re-renders (scripts are not re-executed; only `route()` re-runs), which is exactly the survival mechanism: every re-render rebuilds the panel DOM from `chatTranscripts[entityId]`.
5457:./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5486:orchestration/tickets/T15-day-review/impl-review-raw.txt:2438:   522	1. **Mid-interaction WS flush wipes picker/textarea state (Review).** Any unrelated event (dispatcher tick, another agent's proposal) flushes → full re-render → the grant picker resets and the quick-edit re-prefills. **Decision: acceptable v1.** Rationale: the re-render-everything doctrine is T14 law; preserving per-card edit state would need a keyed store invalidated on proposal supersession — machinery §15's spirit rejects for v1. The review surface is one-at-a-time with short dwell; the human's own actions flush only *after* the card resolves. Recorded here so stage-6 flake triage starts from "known, accepted".
5475:./orchestration/tickets/W3a-status-systemb/plan-review.out:5506:orchestration/tickets/T15-day-review/impl-review-raw.txt:7123:orchestration/tickets/T15-day-review/impl-review-raw.txt:2438:   522	1. **Mid-interaction WS flush wipes picker/textarea state (Review).** Any unrelated event (dispatcher tick, another agent's proposal) flushes → full re-render → the grant picker resets and the quick-edit re-prefills. **Decision: acceptable v1.** Rationale: the re-render-everything doctrine is T14 law; preserving per-card edit state would need a keyed store invalidated on proposal supersession — machinery §15's spirit rejects for v1. The review surface is one-at-a-time with short dwell; the human's own actions flush only *after* the card resolves. Recorded here so stage-6 flake triage starts from "known, accepted".
