10579:I found one real race in the continuing path: queued resumes capture a session key before they actually run, so a rotated session key can make the next queued step resume the stale key, and can even split one ticket’s mind across two queue keys. I’m checking the prior F1-F6 wording now so the final verdict maps back to what was accepted.
10661:    55	    key) -> stderr + exit 1. On 2xx: return the parsed JSON body to the caller."""
10696:Keying `MindQueue` by `ticket_id` violates the W1/W3 carry-forward that continuing steps go through a per-session queue. The W1 queue contract says the key is the durable `session_key` at [queue.py](/Users/khushaljagota/.hermes/planning-v2/src/planner/minds/queue.py:6), and W3 says System B must separately serialize kickoff because two step-0 runs have no key. The plan’s 1:1 assumption is not enforced by schema; `chat_session_key` is just nullable text at [db.py](/Users/khushaljagota/.hermes/planning-v2/src/planner/core/db.py:70).  
10697:Fix: keep `MindQueue` keyed by current non-null `session_key` for continuing runs, and add a System-B-owned ticket/kickoff serialization guard for `NULL` keys. Resolve the stored key inside that guard at execution time.
10704:`session_key: str | ... = _UNSET` is a mypy/runtime typing trap, and the planned end write passes `result.session_key` from [runner.py](/Users/khushaljagota/.hermes/planning-v2/src/planner/minds/runner.py:41), which is `str | None`.  
10705:Fix: define a typed sentinel, e.g. `_Unset`/`_UNSET`, and annotate `session_key: str | None | _Unset = _UNSET`; only include `chat_session_key=?` when the value is not `_UNSET`.
